\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


For $S\subseteq\{1,\ldots,n\}$ with $|S|=k$, let $E_S$ be the $n\times k$ matrix selecting the standard coordinate vectors indexed by $S$. An exact positive-semidefinite principal-block decomposition is an identity $Q=\sum_{|S|=k}E_S\Pi_SE_S^{\mathsf T}$ with real symmetric $\Pi_S\succeq0$. Exact means there is no remaining positive-semidefinite residual. The identity necessarily makes $Q$ real symmetric and positive semidefinite.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Given a matrix $Q$ and an integer $k>2$, find necessary and sufficient conditions for $Q$ to admit an exact $k\times k$ decomposition into positive-semidefinite principal-block contributions. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2019/26}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record open. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Characterize which positive-semidefinite matrices can be assembled exactly from positive-semidefinite blocks supported on $k$ coordinates.

